Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Figure. ",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "Rate of semantic change vs. frequency (\[Minus]log mean rank). Red ",
 "markers are decile means."
}], "Caption",
 CellMargins->{{55, 55}, {2, 
  16}},ExpressionUUID->"787f36e4-2d3a-4831-9fb8-7440be1749c4"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The law of innovation \[Dash] a cautionary tale", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"0ada7abc-9b58-4615-88a8-16a6c5178217"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The published law says ",
 StyleBox["more polysemous words change faster",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". In this corpus the ",
 StyleBox["raw",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " correlation comes out the wrong way: change-",
 "rate vs. polysemy has Spearman \[Rho] = ",
 "-0.31",
 " \[Dash] negative. The culprit is a ",
 StyleBox["confound",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ": polysemous words tend to ",
 "be frequent (\[Rho] = ",
 "0.34",
 "), and frequency strongly suppresses change. In the raw comparison the \
frequency ",
 "effect simply dominates \[Dash] a textbook Simpson's paradox."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"480bc616-b0f4-45f3-969e-916f81cae6fa"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Controlling for frequency (partial Spearman correlation, holding frequency ",
 "rank fixed) the spurious negative trend vanishes \[Dash] but it does not \
turn ",
 "convincingly positive either: partial \[Rho] = ",
 "0.01",
 ", essentially zero. ",
 StyleBox["So in the Google-N-grams eng-all corpus, with WordNet sense counts \
as the polysemy measure, the law of innovation does not replicate.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " That is a more honest \[Dash] and more useful \[Dash] result than forcing \
the sign: ",
 "the law may need a different corpus, a better polysemy measure, or the \
original ",
 "authors' frequency-and-polysemy-matched controls to surface."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"33b20729-98e5-43f9-b847-bae034de7f90"],

